WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.453
A positive whole-number exponent counts repeated factors

00:00:03.453 --> 00:00:09.124
Three to the fourth uses four factors of three, not four added threes

00:00:09.124 --> 00:00:15.667
These repeated multiplications grow the sequence from three to nine, twenty seven, and eighty one.

00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:21.658
Multiplying powers with the same base joins their lists of factors

00:00:21.658 --> 00:00:27.202
Two copies of three followed by three more copies produce five copies altogether

00:00:27.202 --> 00:00:31.467
That is why multiplication adds exponents when the bases match.

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:37.430
Extend the pattern backward by dividing by three at every step

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.397
After three comes one, then one third

00:00:40.397 --> 00:00:49.300
This explains zero and negative exponents consistently: a nonzero base to zero is one, and a negative power means a reciprocal.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:56.689
A power of a sum repeats the whole sum, so its products include two cross terms

00:00:56.689 --> 00:01:04.300
This tiled square shows why x plus one squared contains twice x, unlike the valid power rule for a product.
